         "1. Leaf adaptation: broad leaves are reduced to sharp, slender spines; eliminates stomatal surface area, reducing transpirational water loss to near zero.\n2. Stem adaptation 1 (Water storage): stem tissue is converted into spongy, expandable parenchyma that stores vast water volumes during rain.\n3. Stem adaptation 2 (Photosynthesis): thick green cortex contains chlorophyll, taking over 100% of the plant's photosynthetic carbohydrate production.\n4. Waxy cuticle: stem surface is coated with a thick, glossy, hydrophobic waxy cuticle preventing evaporative leakage.\n5. Root adaptation: extensive shallow radial root network spreads meters wide just below the soil surface to capture brief desert rain showers before water evaporates.",
