         "1. Mammalian identity: warm-blooded, nurse young with milk, possess lungs; completely lack gills.\n2. Blowhole anatomy: nostrils migrated evolutionarily to the apex of the skull, forming single or paired blowholes sealed by muscular plugs underwater.\n3. Breathing cycle: whale surfaces, blows out warm humid air and mucus (the visible oceanic spout), inhales fresh air in seconds, and seals blowholes before submerging.\n4. Deep diving adaptations: high blood volume, myoglobin-rich dark muscles storing massive oxygen reserves, and voluntary slowing of heart rate (bradycardia).\n5. Asphyxiation hazard: if trapped under thick sea ice or tangled in fishing nets unable to reach the surface, a whale will drown.",
