         "1. Broad lamina architecture: large circular leaves act like rafts, spreading leaf weight over a large water surface area to capture full sunlight.\n2. Super-hydrophobic waxy cuticle: lotus leaf micro-papillae coated with epicuticular wax produce the 'Lotus Effect'; water beads into spherical droplets that roll off, carrying dirt away and keeping upper stomata dry and functional.\n3. Upper stomatal distribution: stomata are located strictly on the upper aerial epidermis facing the sky (epistomatous leaf).\n4. Petiole aerenchyma: long leaf stalks contain continuous spongy air-filled chambers (aerenchyma lacunae) providing immense hydrostatic buoyancy.\n5. Submerged anchorage: flexible petioles extend down to roots anchored securely in the muddy nutrient-rich lake bed.",
